<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ktg" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Gas Laws</title>
  <introduction>
  
    <p>
      The gas laws are a set of mathematical relationships that describe the behavior of gases under different conditions. 
      The <term>Boyle's law</term> states that the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume at constant 
      temperature. This means that if the volume of a gas decreases, the pressure increases, and vice versa. 
      The <term>Charles's law</term> states that the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its 
      temperature at constant pressure. This means that if the temperature of a gas increases, the volume 
      increases, and vice versa. The <term>Gay-Lussac's law</term> states that the pressure of a gas is 
      directly proportional to its temperature at constant volume. This means that if the temperature of a 
      gas increases, the pressure increases, and vice versa. The <term>ideal gas law</term> combines 
      these three laws into a single equation, <m>PV = NRT</m>, where <m>P</m> is the pressure of the gas, 
      <m>V</m> is the volume of the gas, <m>N</m> is the number of moles of the gas, <m>R</m> is the ideal gas 
      constant, and <m>T</m> is the temperature of the gas. The ideal gas law is a useful tool for predicting 
      the behavior of gases under a wide range of conditions, although it is important to note that it is an 
      approximation and may not accurately describe the behavior of real gases under certain conditions, 
      such as high pressure or low temperature. 
    </p>
    <p>
      From kinetic theory of gases, we know energy is proportional to temperature. 
     <md>E \propto T</md>

      <md>or, \, PV \propto T</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <term> Boyle's Law: </term> 
      <md>
        V = \frac{f_1(T)}{P}, \, f_1(T) \, \quad \text{ is a function of temperature, T.}
      </md>
       <term> Charles's Law: </term> 
      <md>
        \frac{V}{T} = f_2(P), \, f_2(P) \,\quad  \text{ is a function of pressure, P.}
      </md>
     <term>Avogadro's law:</term> states that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain 
     the same number of molecules. This means that the volume of a gas is directly proportional to the number of molecules. 
     <md>
      V=n \frac{f_1(T)}{P}
     </md>
     At constant temperature and pressure, the volume of a gas is directly proportional to the amount of the gas.
    </p>
    <p>
      By comparing these laws, we get -
      <md number="yes">
        PV = nRT
      </md>
      This is the ideal gas law, where <m>P</m> is the pressure of the gas, <m>V</m> is the volume of the gas,
      <m>n</m> is the number of moles of the gas, <m>R=8.314 \, \text{J/mol/K} </m> <m>(or, \, Pa m^3 K^{-1} mol^{-1}) 
        = \, 0.0821 </m> <m> \, \text{L·atm/mol/K} = 0.08314 bar L K^{-1} mol^{-1}</m> is the ideal gas constant, and <m>T</m> is the temperature of the gas. 
      The ideal gas law is a useful tool for predicting the behavior of gases under a wide range of conditions, 
      although it is important to note that it is an approximation and may not accurately describe the behavior of real 
      gases under certain conditions, such as high pressure or low temperature. Hence, modification is 
      needed in an ideal gas equation to account for the behavior of real gases. 
      The <term>van der Waals equation</term> is a modified version of the ideal gas law that takes into account 
      the volume occupied by gas molecules and the attractive forces between them. 
    </p>

    <p>
      For ideal gas,. internal energy is just the function of absolute temperatrue.
    </p>
    <proof>
      <p>
        From I law of Thermodynamics
        <md>
          \,dQ =\,dU + P\,dV
        </md>
        
        <md number="yes">
            <mrow xml:id="ieq_1"> or, \,dU =\,dQ - P\,dV </mrow>
           </md>
           From II law of thermodynamics 
           <md>
            \,dS = \frac{\,dQ}{T}
           </md>
           
         <md number="yes">
           <mrow xml:id="ieq_2"> or, \,dU =T\,dS - P\,dV </mrow>
           </md>
        Take <m>U =U(V, T) </m> then
        <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_3">  
            \,dU = \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V} \right)_T \,dV + \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T} \right)_V \,dT 
           </mrow>
        </md>
        Also <m>S = S(V, T)</m>
        <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_4">  
            \,dS = \left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \right)_T \,dV + \left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial T} \right)_V \,dT 
           </mrow>
        </md>
        Hence, from <xref ref="ieq_2"/> and <xref ref="ieq_4"/>, we have -
        <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_5"> 
              \,dU = T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial T} \right)_V \,dT + \left[T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \right)_T - P \right] \,dV 
          </mrow>
        </md>
         Also, from <xref ref="ieq_3"/> and <xref ref="ieq_5"/>, we have -
        <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_6"> 
              \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T} \right)_V  = T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial T} \right)_V 
          </mrow>
         
            <mrow xml:id="ieq_7"> 
              \text { and }\quad  \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V} \right)_T  = \left[T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \right)_T - P \right] 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Now, Take <m>F = U - TS</m>
        <md>
           dA = dU - T\, dS - S\, dT = T\,dS - P\,dV - T\, dS -S\, dT  
        </md>
        using <xref ref="ieq_2"/>, Hence, 
        <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_8">  
           or, \,  \,dF = -S\,dT -P\,dV  
          </mrow>
        </md>
        But, <m>F = F(V, T)</m>
         <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_9">  
           or, \,  \,dF = \left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial V} \right)_T \,dV  + \left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial T} \right)_V \,dT 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Compare <xref ref="ieq_8"/> and <xref ref="ieq_9"/>, we have -
         <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_10">  
          \left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial V} \right)_T = -P  
          </mrow>
        </md>
        and, 
          <md number="yes">
          <mrow xml:id="ieq_11">  
         \left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial T} \right)_V  = -S 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Differential <xref ref="ieq_10"/> with respect to <m>T</m> and <xref ref="ieq_11"/> with respect to <m>V</m>, we get -
         <md>
             \left(\frac{\partial^2 F}{\partial V\partial T} \right)_V  =  \left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \right)_T = \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T} \right)_V
           </md>
            <md number="yes">
              <mrow xml:id="ieq_12"> 
         \left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \right)_T = \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T} \right)_V
         </mrow>
           </md>
           From <xref ref="ieq_7"/>
           <md number="yes">
              <mrow xml:id="ieq_13"> 
         \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V} \right)_T = \left[T\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T} \right)_V - P\right]
         </mrow>
           </md>
           Simce, for an ideal gas, 
           <md>
            P=\frac{nRT}{V}
           </md>
         Differentiating it w. r. t. 'T', at constant <m>V</m> we get -
           <md>
            \left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T} \right)_V  = \frac{nR}{V}
           </md>
           Therefore from <xref ref="ieq_13"/>
           <md>
             \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V} \right)_T = \left[\frac{nRT}{V}- P\right] = P-P =0
           </md>
           That is, internal energy is not a function of volume, it only depends on temperature.
      </p>
      
    </proof>
    <p>
       If the volume of an enclosed ideal gas doubles at constant temperature its internal energy does not change.
    </p>
   
  </introduction>
  
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ktg">
    <title>Kinetic Theory of Gases</title>
    <p>
      Bernoulli showed that using the basic concepts of force and randomness, we can relate the pressure of a gas to the molecular motion.   </p>
      <p>
        Suppose the properties of molecules moving in one direction will be the same as the properties of molecules moving in anyother direction, then we can assume 
        the average speed of the gas molecules is <m>v_{ave}</m>. Where 
        <md number="yes">
          v^2_{ave} =\bar{v}_{x}^2 +\bar{v}_{y}^2 +\bar{v}_{z}^2=\bar{v}^2
        </md>
        Therefore all directions are equivalent, we must write 
         <md number="yes">
          \bar{v}_{x}^2 =\bar{v}_{y}^2 =\bar{v}_{z}^2=\frac{\bar{v}^2}{3}
        </md>
        Note: We squared it because we take magnitude of velocity.
      </p>
      <p>
        Consider N mole of gas in a cube, where 
        <m>n=</m> number of mole of gas, <m>V=</m> gas volume, <m>M=</m> molar mass of the gas, <m>m=\frac{M}{N_A}</m> = mass of one molecule, 
        <m>\frac{N}{V}=\frac{nN_A}{V}</m> = number of molecules per unit volume, <m>N_A=</m> Avagadro's number.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>F_x</m> be the force exerts on the wall of the container of volume <m>V=A\Delta x</m> when a molecule hits the wall along x-axis.
        <md>
          \therefore \, F_x= \frac{\Delta p_x}{\Delta t} = \frac{m\bar{v}_x-(-m\bar{v}_x )}{\Delta t}\times \frac{N}{2}A\Delta x
        </md>
        The factor <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> is taken because half of the molecules are moving along x-axis and half of them are moving opposite way. Now,
        <md number="yes"> 
          P_x=\frac{F_x}{A} = 2m\bar{v}^2_x \frac{N}{2} = mN\bar{v}^2_x 
        </md>
        Since, direction x is arbitrary, it is better to write, 
        <md>
          P=\frac{1}{3}mN\bar{v}^2 = \frac{1}{3}\frac{M}{N_A}\frac{nN_A}{V}\bar{v}^2 = \frac{1}{3}\frac{Mn}{V}\bar{v}^2 
        </md>
         <md number="yes">
          or, \, PV = \frac{1}{3}Mn\bar{v}^2 
        </md>
        <md>
          But, \, PV = nRT =  \frac{1}{3}Mn\bar{v}^2 
        </md>
          <md number="yes">
          or, \, RT =  \frac{1}{3}M\bar{v}^2 
        </md>
        Using Boltzmann constant, 
        <md>
          k_B = \frac{R}{N_A} = 1.3807\times 10^{-23} JK^{-1}
        </md>
         <md>
          k_B N_A T =  \frac{1}{3}mN_A\bar{v}^2 
        </md>
          <md>
         or, \,  k_B T =  \frac{1}{3}m\bar{v}^2 
        </md>
         <md number="yes">
         or, \,  \frac{1}{2}m\bar{v}^2  =  \frac{3}{2}k_B T  
        </md>
        This equation relates temperature to molecular motion. From equation of RT, 
        we can also see that the total kinetic energy of 1 mole of gas equals <m>\frac{3}{2}RT</m>
      </p>


 <image width="40%" xml:id="particle_box">
  
  <latex-image >
 \begin{tikzpicture}
     \filldraw (0.4,-0.5)  circle (1pt);
    \node at (0.5, -2.2) {$\Delta x$};
    \draw (-0.5,0) rectangle (2,-2); 
    \draw [-latex,dashed] (0.4,-0.5) -- (1.95,-0.95) -- (1.4,-1.3); 
    \node at (2.2,-1) {$A$};
  \end{tikzpicture}

  </latex-image>
    </image>

  </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-eqpt">
  <title>Equipartition Theorem</title>
  
  <p>
    The energy associated with each independent mode of vibration (motion) is <m>k_BT/2</m>. 
    For example: For a point particle, there are three independent mode of motion, i.e., translational motion (monoatomic gas, <m>He, \, Ar</m>), 
    so degrees of freeedom <m>f=3</m>, The K.E. for this particle is <m>\frac{3}{2}k_B T</m>
  </p>
<image width="20%" xml:id="eq_part-1">
  
  <latex-image >
 \begin{tikzpicture}
     \filldraw (0,0)  circle (1pt);
    \draw [-latex,dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,1,0) node [above] {$z$} ; 
     \draw [-latex,dashed] (0,0,0) -- (1,0,0) node [above] {$y$}; 
      \draw [-latex,dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1) node [above] {$x$}; 
     \end{tikzpicture}

  </latex-image>
    </image>
  
    <p>
      For a diatomic particle  <m>(H2,\, O_2)</m>, degrees of freeedom <m>f=6-1=5</m>, The K.E. for this particle is <m>\frac{5}{2}k_B T</m>
    </p>
    <image width="20%" xml:id="eq_part-2">
  
  <latex-image >
 \begin{tikzpicture}
    \filldraw  (0,0)  circle (1pt);
      \filldraw  (1,0)  circle (1pt);
     % \draw (0,0) -- (1,0);
    \draw [-latex,dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,1,0) node [above] {$z$} ; 
     \draw [-latex,dashed] (1,0,0) -- (1,1,0)  ; 
     \draw [dashed] (0,0,0) -- (1,0,0) node at (0.5,0.1,0) {$y$}; 
      \draw [-latex,dashed] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1) node [above] {$x$}; 
        \draw [-latex,dashed] (1,0,0) -- (1,0,1) ; 
     \end{tikzpicture}

  </latex-image>
    </image>
    <p>
      The independent mode of motion is called <em>degree of freedom.</em>
    </p>
     <p>
      For a triatomic particle  <m>(O_3,\, -- )</m>, degrees of freeedom <m>f=9-2=7</m>, in linear arrangement. 
    </p>
   <image width="20%" xml:id="eq_part-3">
  
  <latex-image >
 \begin{tikzpicture}
    \filldraw  (0,0)  circle (1pt)    (1,0)  circle (1pt)  (2,0)  circle (1pt) ;
     
 \draw [dashed] (0,0) -- (2,0);  
 
     \end{tikzpicture}

  </latex-image>
    </image>

     <p>
      For a triatomic particle  <m>(O_3,\, -- )</m>, in triangular arrangement the degrees of freeedom <m>f=9-3=6</m>.  
    </p>
   
     <image width="20%" xml:id="eq_part-4">
  
  <latex-image >
 \begin{tikzpicture}
     \filldraw  (0,-1)  circle (1pt)    (-1,-2)  circle (1pt)  (1,-2)  circle (1pt) ;
   \draw [dashed] (0,-1) -- (-1,-2) -- (1,-2) -- cycle;  
     \end{tikzpicture}

  </latex-image>
    </image>
 <p>
      The other mode of motion is a contribution of rational and vibrational motion.
    </p>
</subsection>


</section>